@meta
id: 03-06
title: Drill — above and below
chapter: 03
type: drill
skills: open-o, open-O
reps: 8
par: 50
minutes: 5
vscode: click, Enter, type, click, Enter, type
open: release.md

@brief
Eight times: add `- [ ] merge main` above the line that is there, and
`- [ ] run the tests` below it. One line above, one line below, and the
existing line untouched in the middle.

@keys
| `O` | open a line above |
| `o` | open a line below |
| `j` | down one line |
| `Esc` | out |

@hints
`O` first while you are still on the original line, then `Esc`. You are
now on the new top line, so you need to come back down before you open
the one below.
--
Do not count lines or think about order the second time round. Aim for a
single unbroken run: `O` … `Esc` `j` `o` … `Esc` `:wq`.

@solution
    O - [ ] merge main <Esc> j o - [ ] run the tests <Esc> :wq<Enter>

Forty-four keystrokes for two new lines, thirty-five of them text.

The `j` in the middle is the only thing to watch. After `O` and `Esc` you
are standing on the line you just made, not the line you started on, so
the "below" in `o` would be the wrong below. Vim leaves the cursor on the
text you just typed, always. Once you expect that, you stop being
surprised by where the next command lands.

Repeat this one until you can do the whole thing without reading the
screen in the middle.

@start release.md
- [ ] run the migrations

@expected release.md
- [ ] merge main
- [ ] run the migrations
- [ ] run the tests
